\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $K_1,K_2,K_3$ be pairwise disjoint closed subsets of the Riemann sphere $\widehat{\mathbb C}$ and $C_1,C_2,C_3\in\mathbb C$. On $K=\bigcup_iK_i$ put $f|_{K_i}=C_i$. For $n\ge0$ let $R_n$ be the rational functions of degree at most $n$: $p/q$ in reduced form with $\max(\deg p,\deg q)\le n$. Only functions finite on $K$, including at infinity when it belongs to $K$, can have finite approximation error. Set \[\rho_n(f)=\inf_{r\in R_n}\sup_{z\in K}|f(z)-r(z)|.\]
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Find a geometric characterization of the exponential approximation rate \[\lim_{n\to\infty}\rho_n(f)^{1/n},\] including the existence and degenerate cases of this limit, in terms of the three sets and their assigned constants. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Describe geometrically the exponential rate of best rational approximation to a function constant on three disjoint closed sphere sets.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2309005 (AMR-022-9005). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Walter K. Hayman and Edward F. Lingham, \emph{Research Problems in Function Theory} (2018), arXiv:1809.07200. Problem 9.5, complete original question, all following updates, and the relevant chapter definitions. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}.
\end{description}
